  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSecondary cold front and potent upper level shortwave continue\ntracking through the region early this afternoon, accompanied by\ngusty rain showers. Several NYS Mesonet obs indicated peak wind\ngusts of 35-45 mph as these showers passed, and KALB measured \n43 KT (~50 mph).\n\nBehind the cold front and shortwave, deeper mixing should still\nallow for occasional wind gusts of 30-40 mph through sunset,\nstrongest and most persistent within the Mohawk Valley, Capital\nRegion, northern/central Taconics and Berkshires. Temps will\nlikely hold steady or fall in many areas through this afternoon.\n\nThe combination of occasional clouds and some wind overnight\nwill limit the cooling potential somewhat, with min temps \ndropping into the upper 20s to mid 30s across higher elevations,\nand mid/upper 30s to lower 40s in valleys. There could be a \nbrief period of light winds toward daybreak across portions of \nthe upper Hudson Valley, allowing for a few sheltered locations \nto drop into the lower/mid 30s.\n\nAfter a mostly sunny and cool day on Tuesday, nearly ideal\nradiational cooling should allow temps to drop off rapidly after\nsunset. However, some high/mid level clouds and a developing\nlight breeze may slow down rate of falling temps across the \nAdirondacks/Mohawk Valley after midnight. Still, expect at least\nareas of frost to occur in areas where the growing season \nremains (Hudson River Valley, Dutchess and Litchfield counties),\nand frost advisories will likely be needed for most of these \nareas, with the possibility of some freeze warnings across \nportions of the upper Hudson Valley (Lake George/Saratoga \nregion).\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAfter a cold start, a developing southwest flow will bring more\nseasonable temperatures for Wednesday afternoon which should\nthen continue into Thursday. A moisture-starved frontal system\nmay bring some light rain showers or sprinkles to some areas\nmainly north and west of Albany Thursday into Friday, otherwise\nmainly dry conditions are expected into next weekend.",
